{
  "dom_id": "question-184671",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Suppose we wish to construct a proof by contrapositive of the following statement:\n\nLet $n$ be an integer. If ${n}^{2}-2n$ is odd, then $n$ is odd.\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\nThe contrapositive form of the statement is as follows:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,⇒\\,{{field-2}}\n$$\n\nIf $n$ is {{field-3}}, then $n=$ {{field-4}}, where $a$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nSubstituting {{field-6}} into the expression ${n}^{2}-2n,$ we can show that ${n}^{2}-2n=$ {{field-7}}, where $b$ is {{field-8}}.\n\nTherefore, since ${n}^{2}-2n$ {{field-9}} a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that ${n}^{2}-2n$ is {{field-10}}.",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
